# PART 5 — MOVIE DISCOVERY AND SEARCH ENGINE

This is a critical priority.

CINEMIND must support global movie discovery with accurate filters.

## A. Search modes

Support:
1. Exact movie-title search.
2. Partial movie-title search.
3. Original-language discovery.
4. Genre-based discovery.
5. Actor search.
6. Director search.
7. Keyword search.
8. Natural-language movie search.
9. Similar-movie discovery.
10. Release-period discovery.

Use the appropriate API operation for the task.

For TMDB:

- Use /search/movie for text-based title and alternative-title search.
- Use /discover/movie for structured filtering.
- Use movie details and appended data for individual movie information.

Do not use a title search endpoint as a replacement for structured discovery.

## B. Correct language filtering

Distinguish:

- Original language.
- Displayed metadata language.
- Dubbed language availability.
- Subtitle language availability.
- Regional release country.

These are not interchangeable.

Use the source's original-language filter.

For Tamil, use the TMDB original-language code ta.

For other languages, use the provider's supported language codes.

Do not assume every language has identical metadata coverage.

Use the supported language configuration instead of maintaining an incomplete hardcoded language map.

### Mandatory strict-filter behaviour

When a user explicitly asks for Tamil movies:
- Apply the Tamil original-language constraint at the backend.
- Return matching Tamil original-language movies only.
- Do not silently include Telugu, Hindi, Malayalam or English films.
- Do not remove the language constraint when the first page has no results.
- Continue pagination within the valid filter where appropriate.
- If no matching results exist, show an honest empty state.

If the user asks for Tamil-dubbed movies, use a separate dubbed-availability workflow. Do not treat original-language Tamil movies as the only possible result.

## C. Correct release-period filtering

Support:
- Latest releases.
- This week.
- This month.
- This year.
- Specific year.
- Custom date range.
- Classics.
- All-time best.

For latest-release queries, use appropriate release-date constraints.

For regional theatrical releases, use the correct region and release type where supported.

Never return old films as new releases simply because they are popular.

Do not apply a recent-release constraint to classic-film or all-time searches.

## D. Relevance and ranking

Use a transparent ranking model.

Consider:
- Exact title match.
- Original-language match.
- Genre match.
- Release-period match.
- Runtime match.
- User-selected filters.
- Source popularity.
- Rating quality.
- Vote count.

Explicit user constraints must take precedence over general popularity.

Never broaden a strict filter without informing the user and obtaining permission.

## E. Search diagnostics

During development, record:
- Query received.
- Parsed intent.
- Selected language code.
- Selected region.
- Selected date range.
- API endpoint.
- Query parameters.
- Number of results.
- Filter rejection counts.
- Pagination status.
- Fallback reason.

This is required to debug incorrect search results.

